            B.q("ai-m3-23", "ai-cosmos-changefeed", "The handler that copies changed orders into a search index sometimes receives the same change twice. Which two design choices keep the index correct? Select two.", listOf("Write to the index with an upsert keyed by the item id, so a repeat overwrites instead of duplicating", "Store the item's _etag or version with the copy and skip changes you have already applied", "Delete the lease container after each batch", "Assume exactly-once delivery and add nothing"), setOf(0, 1), "A Cosmos DB trigger can receive the same batch of changes more than once, and Microsoft says this is by design. It happens when a function-level retry policy re-runs a failed batch, when a run goes past its allowed execution time, or when leases are rebalanced as instances scale out or in. (By default the trigger saves its lease checkpoint after every function call, even one that failed, and does not retry a failed batch unless you add a retry policy.) So make the handler idempotent, meaning safe to run twice: upsert by the item's id and partition key, or skip versions you have already applied.",
                scenario = "Marlow Books mirrors its orders container into a search store through a Cosmos DB trigger.",
